<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sakya Pandita</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1182–1251</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sakya Pandita Kunga Gyaltsen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sakya Pandita, Kunga Gyaltsen, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> scholar and one of the five founding masters of the Sakya school. Famous for his learning in logic, grammar and poetry, he answered the summons of the Mongol prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Köten</a> in 1244 and met him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Liangzhou</a> in 1247, negotiating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>'s submission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. His nephew Phagpa later became Imperial Preceptor to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sakya-pandita.jpg" alt="Sakya Pandita" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MET_2006_285.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
